WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:06.779
Film Courage: Before we started with our interview
we were talking about hiring people and people

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:08.310
reacting to the word No.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.290
People reacting to the word Yes.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:14.030
Can we talk about this and what it means to
you?

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:19.060
Rachel K. Ofori, Producer/Writer: Yes, a little
practice that I have adapted and I still play

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:26.539
around with is when hiring someone or working
with someone or going into any place of partnership

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:32.489
or place where I will have to interact with
someone, I watch the way they respond to No.

00:00:32.489 --> 00:00:37.179
Even if I have to kind of strum up an opportunity
where I can tell them No early on or even

00:00:37.179 --> 00:00:43.809
if it’s not a No it’s like Alright, give
me a second, whatever it is.

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:46.980
Now in those early stages were you are not
going to come out and say these things.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.780
You have to be very, very, very perceptive
but just see how they react to that and that

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.930
is going to usually be a tell-tale sign to
something about their character that you might

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.050
have to keep an eye on.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:01.559
I say I play around with it because I’ve
actually seen this work where I have never

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:02.559
been wrong.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:07.469
It’s like Okay I saw the way they responded
to No even a year from then they are having

00:01:07.469 --> 00:01:15.289
a horrible situation with a co-worker or cast
member or this or that and it comes down to

00:01:15.289 --> 00:01:20.840
that one thing where I saw they can’t handle
the No.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.990
Or there is something that stirs up in them
from a No.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:29.829
And what I’ve seen is you can simplify it
to be in childhood of maybe Mom told them

00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:34.719
No too much or maybe Mom told them No not
enough or ever?

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:38.479
Or the teacher was kind of like Well they
are a special kid.

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:42.670
Let’s let them do what they want or whatever
it is where you had freedom to do something

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:47.159
that maybe wasn’t the most like healthy
freedom where you needed some boundaries or

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:50.630
whatever it is.

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:57.350
Some people are then able to mature and seek
out what they didn’t get but most people

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.350
aren’t.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.600
Most of us just aren’t that mature.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:00.909
It just is what it is.

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:06.440
So we are going to fall into a space that
we were prone to from childhood.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.970
Even if you don’t see it in certain moments
you are going to see it when things get bad.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:13.270
And with film, things are going to get bad
at least one day.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:16.209
It’s like the moment of truth.

00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:19.350
You get to know people so much after you film
and film with people.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.780
Everyone is like like Oh, I love you and whenever
that happened you work with someone for a

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.330
year and you love them.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:29.900
You can work at a plant, I don’t know you
are like What are your kids names?

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.730
You don’t know anything about a person,
you don’t have any feelings for the person

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.060
but the feelings are high because you’re
very connected, it’s intimate spaces, it’s

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:42.980
long days, it’s things that are uncontrollable
like weather and finance and whatever.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.060
Things that you just can’t control.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:54.510
So it’s going to stir up people in a more
aggressive way than other vehicles right?

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.430
Knowing that you need to know how they respond
to No.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:03.430
You need to know how they respond to an obstacle
and you need to know how they are going to

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:08.370
interact when they are upset about something
or not happy about something or didn’t like

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:09.980
something.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:16.030
The way that I find that out in a very limited
way is to early on even before I hire them

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:20.910
to tell then No or Wait or I don’t really
like when you said that.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.830
How do they respond to that?

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:28.520
Scott who I work with he is very much a part
of our company The Audacity but also he directed

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:35.190
the film I last worked on and he is like the
best at I don’t really like that and he

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:36.610
is like Oh, why don’t you like it?

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.800
That’s his response, that’s awesome.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.520
It’s not How dare you!

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:41.960
It’s like No it’s okay.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:42.960
Now let’s talk.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.490
I don’t like that, you don’t like that,
why?

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:49.600
But if someone is like I don’t like when
you said such-and-such or I don’t really

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:50.600
like that idea.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.400
And they get sad, it doesn’t mean don’t
work with them it just means be mindful that

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.840
this person may not be able to take those
moments and you are going to have to have

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.550
those moments when being creative.

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:05.690
Creatives have to feel confident to talk and
they can’t be shut down by a No.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:08.510
So there is someone I am working with now,
brilliant!

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:12.210
But the No’s or when the other team is like
What about this?

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.010
I don’t really like that idea, what about
this?

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.380
You see them pulling back and kind of putting
a wall up and digging into that a little bit

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:23.460
more they didn’t have those opportunities
to kind of like show themselves as a kid and

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.030
be validated by showing themselves.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.930
It was more like Stay in your place, no not
right now.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.020
No, stay here.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:34.620
So when that person starts to feel a little
comfortable and they say what they were thinking

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.420
I need the team to be mindful now is not the
time to criticize.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:42.270
Accept what they just said and then we’re
going to deliver this a little more softer

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:43.780
than I would with Scott.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.490
Scott I don’t like it.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:46.670
Okay, why?

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:51.230
It’s being mindful of people, being careful
with it but knowing that this is going to

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:52.230
be necessary.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.180
So if it’s necessary for me to tell you
I don’t like your idea let’s find out

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:59.600
how you react to that earlier than like Oh
my gosh, we had a deadline and this person

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.640
will literally not write now because now they
are having a mental breakdown because they

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.830
don’t like their idea because that can derail
a whole project.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:13.290
So finding that out early on in a fun little
way, a very easy way that I have adapted to

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:18.130
my own likes is yes, finding out how they
respond to No or Wait or I don’t like it.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:18.150
Question For The Viewers: Do you pay attention
to the way people around you handle No?

